{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Rule 6c-11(b) provides four conditional exemptions for eligible U.S. ETFs:\n\n1. **Affiliated-person transactions:** relief from Investment Company Act §17(a)(1) and (2) for certain in-kind creation-unit transactions with affiliates.\n2. **Affiliated index-based funds:** relief from §12(d)(1)(A) and (B) for specified acquisitions involving an ETF and affiliated funds.\n3. **Pro-rata in-kind redemptions:** relief from §17(a)(1) and (2) for in-kind redemptions to an affiliate when the basket is pro rata (subject to limited cash substitutions).\n4. **Delayed foreign investment delivery:** relief from §22(e)’s seven-day redemption-payment limit where foreign-market delivery is delayed.\n\nThe affiliate relief is not a blanket permission for conflicted trades: it is limited to qualifying creation/redemption transactions, subject to the rule’s conditions and procedures designed to prevent favoritism or dilution. For delayed foreign investments, the delay must arise from local-market delivery cycles or market holidays, not ETF discretion; the fund must deliver the foreign investment as soon as practicable, and in all cases within 15 days after tender."}}
